Hello again,

I have a question about interpreting pcount results. I am assuming
that the "state" abundance estimates are in log-link scale and need to
be back transformed. The 'predict' function does this for you, if I am
correct, but my abundance estimates per site are very high (16-60) and
my detection is very low (0.01-0.05) when ~25% of sites were observed
as occupied by 1 to 8 individuals. My data does not seem to be very
over-dispersed and both the Negative binomial and Poisson mixture
yields these high estimates. Am I misinterpreting the output?

> summary(m101)

Call:
pcount(formula = ~obsNum ~ upland + lowland + agri, data = c10,
    K = 100, mixture = "NB", method = "BFGS", engine = c("C"))

Abundance (log-scale):
            Estimate     SE      z P(>|z|)
(Intercept)  5.58483 1.9647  2.843 0.00448
upland      -0.02546 0.0217 -1.174 0.24023
lowland     -0.03024 0.0227 -1.334 0.18213
agri        -0.00824 0.0233 -0.353 0.72404

Detection (logit-scale):
            Estimate    SE      z  P(>|z|)
(Intercept)   -3.897 0.227 -17.17 4.61e-66
obsNum2        0.608 0.227   2.68 7.36e-03
obsNum3       -0.141 0.266  -0.53 5.96e-01
obsNum4        0.531 0.230   2.31 2.08e-02
obsNum5        0.470 0.232   2.02 4.33e-02
obsNum6        0.730 0.222   3.28 1.02e-03

Dispersion (log-scale):
 Estimate    SE    z P(>|z|)
    0.391 0.287 1.36   0.173

AIC: 1024.278
Number of sites: 55
optim convergence code: 0
optim iterations: 67
Bootstrap iterations: 0

> predict(m101,type="state")

   Predicted        SE     lower     upper
1   27.65046  5.796090 18.334687  41.69954
2   31.53736  7.178368 20.187351  49.26873
3   40.37186 12.678125 21.815879  74.71103
4   36.62003  9.499664 22.024553  60.88781
5   40.37024 10.061486 24.769432  65.79706
etc....

Thank you in advance,

- Tyler

